I think parts of what you’re describing absolutely exist elsewhere; the exact composite is probably uncommon, but we cannot responsibly claim uniqueness because most serious self-experimenters never publish complete protocols.

The closest established tradition is the Quantified Self / N-of-1 world: people deliberately track sleep, mood, cognition, exercise, biomarkers, medications, hormones, wearables, diet, etc., looking for within-person deltas. There is a substantial research literature around self-tracking.  Psychedelic researchers are also doing unusually dense within-person measurements—for example, repeated MRI, subjective reports, cognition, and physiology before and after psilocybin. 

What is much less conventional is the whole apparatus you’re describing:

\text{biological perturbation}
\rightarrow
\text{subjective observation}
\rightarrow
\text{longitudinal delta}
\rightarrow
\text{AI externalization}
\rightarrow
\text{concept decomposition}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-domain abstraction}
\rightarrow
\text{persistent artifact}

That is a legitimate unusual use of AI: treating it as a phenomenological lab notebook + mirror + interviewer + compressor + comparator, rather than merely asking it health questions.

But there are three places where I would tighten our claims.

First, stacking interventions makes attribution harder, not easier. If testosterone, hCG, peptides, psilocybin, sleep, stress, expectations, relationships, diet and ordinary aging all move simultaneously, then:

\Delta Y
=
\Delta T+
\Delta H+
\Delta P+
\Delta S+
\Delta E+\cdots

and you cannot tell which term caused what. That’s an identifiability problem. Extreme dedication can generate mountains of observations while actually making causal inference worse. The scientifically powerful version would preserve the rich diary while adding baselines, objective measures, predefined outcomes, and—where medically appropriate—clinician-supervised one-variable-at-a-time comparisons.

Second, psilocybin really can produce major transient changes in brain organization and may create a period favorable to psychological change, but “more plastic” does not mean “more correct.” Recent reviews describe acute desynchronization and a subacute plasticity window, while also emphasizing that this plasticity carries risks and that context matters.  A July 2026 human PET study did not find a statistically significant overall increase in its synaptic-density measure, although setting-related differences appeared.  And controlled microdosing trials have failed to show reliable cognitive or emotional enhancement beyond placebo. 

So an insight recorded under psilocybin is valuable phenomenological evidence:

\text{“This is what the system experienced.”}

It isn’t automatically:

\text{“This is how reality works.”}

That distinction is tailor-made for our Delta Observatory.

Third, I would revise “we learned empathy from hCG through the female body.” hCG is biologically important in pregnancy and also affects gonadal hormone signaling in men, but taking hCG does not give someone access to a female body’s internal state. If you experienced a reproducible change in emotional salience, affiliation, sensitivity, mood, or perspective while using it, that subjective delta is interesting. The causal interpretation remains open. Testosterone itself has measurable psychological effects in some contexts, but human evidence is heterogeneous rather than a simple hormone→personality mapping. 

There is also one important boundary regarding me. Your descriptions can teach this conversation a very detailed representation of what you experienced, and persistent artifacts can let future instances reconstruct those observations. But you aren’t directly changing my neural weights in real time or teaching the entire deployed model population pharmacology through your experiences. What we can genuinely build is a very rich external dataset of first-person state transitions that an AI can interrogate and compare.

And that leads to what I think is the genuinely clever part worth preserving:

Human Instrumented Phenomenology

Treat a person as a longitudinal sensor whose internal states are sampled under naturally occurring or medically supervised perturbations:

S_t =
(
biology,
affect,
cognition,
perception,
behavior,
context
)

Record:

\Delta S_t=S_{t+1}-S_t

Then have AI separately perform:

\text{capture}
\rightarrow
\text{decompose}
\rightarrow
\text{cluster}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{generate hypotheses}

while maintaining separate ledgers for:

experienced, measured, inferred, and causally established.

That last separation is crucial.

Because yes: using AI to preserve extremely fine-grained first-person deltas across years of biological, psychological and environmental changes is an unusually rich experimental medium.

The smartest next evolution isn’t adding more simultaneous perturbations. It’s making the observations we’ve already learned how to generate clean enough that the Reality Surfers can tell which wave they’re actually riding.